2026-05-12 23:16:17,167 - INFO - thoughts: ["### Possible Next Three Steps for Feature Engineering:\n\n#### Step 1: **Create a Water Distance Ratio**\n```python\n# Feature name: Water_Distance_Ratio\n# Feature description: The ratio of Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology divided by the Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology. This describes the relative spatial relationship between the horizontal and vertical distances from water sources.\n# Usefulness: This feature captures terrain slope near hydrology and how steep or flat the terrain near water features is. It might indicate accessibility or proximity to water and may correlate with forest cover type (e.g., denser vegetation on flatter terrain near water bodies).\ndf['Water_Distance_Ratio'] = df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology'] / (df['Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology'] + 1e-6)  # Add a small value to avoid division by zero\n```end\n\n#### Step 2: **Compute Combined Hillshade Metrics**\n```python\n# Feature name: Mean_Hillshade\n# Feature description: The average value of Hillshade_9am, Hillshade_Noon, and Hillshade_3pm.\n# Usefulness: This feature summarizes overall exposure to sunlight across the day, which may affect the growth and survival of different tree species. Areas with lower average sunlight may correspond to more shade-tolerant forest cover types.\ndf['Mean_Hillshade'] = (df['Hillshade_9am'] + df['Hillshade_Noon'] + df['Hillshade_3pm']) / 3\n\n# Feature name: Hillshade_Variability\n# Feature description: The standard deviation of Hillshade_9am, Hillshade_Noon, and Hillshade_3pm.\n# Usefulness: Variability in sunlight throughout the day might indicate features like highly variable terrain, which could affect vegetation differences. High variability may also hint at areas with transitions between different forest cover types or microclimates.\ndf['Hillshade_Variability'] = df[['Hillshade_9am', 'Hillshade_Noon', 'Hillshade_3pm']].std(axis=1)\n```end\n\n#### Step 3: **Create Distance-to-Water Road Interaction**\n```python\n# Feature name: Distance_To_Water_Road_Product\n# Feature description: The product of Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology and Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways. This combines two accessibility-related features into one.\n# Usefulness: This feature captures interaction between water proximity and road proximity, which might affect human activity or forest management. Forest cover near roads and water may differ from areas farther from both, potentially correlating with forest types linked to accessibility and conservation versus remote natural ecosystems.\ndf['Distance_To_Water_Road_Product'] = df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology'] * df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways']\n```end\n\n---\n\n#### Code Snippet for Dropping a Column:\n```python\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type1 is dropped\n# Soil_Type1 is dropped because its values are always 0. It does not provide any variability or useful information for prediction.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type1'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n1. **Water_Distance_Ratio** combines horizontal and vertical hydrology features to capture slope and terrain proximity near water bodies.\n2. **Mean_Hillshade** and **Hillshade_Variability** distill sunlight exposure into useful aggregated metrics relevant to vegetation and terrain.\n3. **Distance_To_Water_Road_Product** integrates human accessibility features—roads and water—for insights into the forest cover types based on accessibility.\n\n### Why These are Useful\n- These feature combinations highlight underlying natural relationships between terrain, hydrology, sunlight exposure, and accessibility that may influence forest cover type.\n- Each feature adds interpretive power and complexity to the model, potentially improving predictions by capturing interactions and reducing model bias due to individually noisy inputs.", '```python\n# Feature 1: Elevation Relative to Vertical Distance to Hydrology\ndf[\'Elevation_Relative_To_Hydrology\'] = df[\'Elevation\'] - df[\'Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology\']\n# Feature description: Calculates the elevation relative to the vertical distance from the nearest hydrological feature. This may indicate how the terrain interacts with water drainage and accumulation, which can influence forest type distribution.\n# Usefulness: Differentiates areas where elevation plays a significant role relative to nearby water features. Forest types may correlate with water availability and runoff patterns.\n\n# Feature 2: Aspect Grouped into Cardinal Directions\ndf[\'Aspect_Categorized\'] = pd.cut(df[\'Aspect\'], bins=[0, 90, 180, 270, 360], labels=[\'N\', \'E\', \'S\', \'W\'], right=False)\ndf = pd.get_dummies(df, columns=[\'Aspect_Categorized\'], prefix=\'Aspect\')\n# Feature description: Converts aspect (direction the slope faces) into cardinal categories (North, East, South, West) and applies one-hot encoding.\n# Usefulness: Simplifies the degrees azimuth into meaningful directional data that may correlate with microclimates or sunlight exposure, influencing vegetation growth.\n\n# Feature 3: Combined Horizontal Distance Features\ndf[\'Distance_To_Important_Features\'] = df[\'Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology\'] + df[\'Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways\'] + df[\'Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points\']\n# Feature description: Computes the combined horizontal distance to hydrological features, roadways, and fire points. This feature captures proximity to human activity and natural resources.\n# Usefulness: Helps understand how distance from human impacts (roadways and fire points) and natural hydrological features interact in shaping forest cover types.\n```end\n\n```python\n# Dropping columns with little variability\ndf.drop(columns=[\'Soil_Type1\', \'Soil_Type5\', \'Soil_Type14\', \'Soil_Type15\', \'Soil_Type37\'], inplace=True)\n# Explanation why the column XX is dropped: These soil type indicators have zero variance (all values are 0) across the dataset, meaning they do not provide any discriminatory informati