ropped: These soil type indicators have zero variance (all values are 0) across the dataset, meaning they do not provide any discriminatory information for prediction.\nend\n\npython\n# Dropping overly redundant columns\ndf.drop(columns=['Aspect'], inplace=True)\n# Explanation why the column Aspect is dropped: The raw Aspect feature has been simplified and transformed into categorized cardinal directions (one-hot encoded). Keeping the original feature would introduce redundancy and dilute the predictive power of the model.\nend\n\nBy engineering these features and dropping selected columns, we create new insights and reduce noise, further improving the downstream task of predicting forest cover type.", "Here are three possible next steps for feature engineering to improve predictions for the forest cover type classification task:\n\npython\n# 1. Elevation to Elevation_Relative_Hydrology Ratio\n# Feature description: Creates a ratio that captures the relative importance of elevation in the context of proximity to hydrology. This could highlight differential relationships between elevation bands and water features that influence vegetation.\n# Usefulness: Combining these two features identifies how terrain interacts with water drainage relative to its elevation. Some vegetation types may prefer certain proportional relationships between elevation and hydrological proximity.\ndf['Elevation_Hydrology_Ratio'] = df['Elevation'] / (df['Elevation_Relative_To_Hydrology'] + 1)\nend\n\npython\n# 2. Combined Horizontal Distance to Roadways and Fire Points\n# Feature description: Computes the sum of distances to human-impact-related features: roadways and fire points. This aggregates the two human factor influences, which may jointly impact forest cover distribution.\n# Usefulness: Both roadways and wildfire points introduce human disturbances. Summing these distances will capture how isolated the location is from human activities, which could correlate with specific forest types.\ndf['Dist_To_Human_Impact'] = df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways'] + df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points']\nend\n\npython\n# 3. Soil Diversity Indicator\n# Feature description: Counts the presence of multiple soil types for a given observation (i.e., summing the soil type indicator variables). This provides a proxy for soil diversity at a given location.\n# Usefulness: Higher soil diversity might indicate transition zones or areas more likely to support multiple vegetation types, which could be predictive of forest cover classification.\ndf['Soil_Diversity'] = df[[col for col in df.columns if 'Soil_Type' in col]].sum(axis=1)\nend\n\n### Columns to be Dropped (if warranted):\n\npython\n# Dropping irrelevant aspect columns\n# Explanation: The categorical values for Aspect_N, Aspect_S, Aspect_E, and Aspect_W could be compiled into a single analytical feature such as circular orientation or eliminated for redundancy.\ndf.drop(columns=['Aspect_N', 'Aspect_S', 'Aspect_E', 'Aspect_W'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Dropping Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology if Elevation_Relative_To_Hydrology proves superior\n# Explanation: Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology is captured implicitly by Elevation_Relative_To_Hydrology, which is already a more processed combination feature. This saves redundancy.\ndf.drop(columns=['Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Dropping extreme low variance soil types\n# Explanation: Indicators such as Soil_Type7, Soil_Type8, and others with extremely rare occurrences (Mean < 0.001) likely do not provide enough distinguishing information and can be dropped to reduce feature space.\nlow_variance_soil_types = [col for col in df.columns if 'Soil_Type' in col and df[col].mean() < 0.001]\ndf.drop(columns=low_variance_soil_types, inplace=True)\nend\n\nBy combining basic features into higher-order or aggregate features like ratios, sums, and counts, while reducing unnecessary columns, these steps aim to improve the downstream task's performance."), with improvements -
