st_To_Human_Impact"])\n```end\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Feature Creation - Soil Diversity Interaction with Slope\n```python\n# Feature name: Soil_Diversity_Slope_Interaction\n# Feature description: Creates an interaction feature by multiplying Soil_Diversity and Slope to capture the combined influence of soil diversity and terrain steepness on vegetation.\n# Usefulness: Certain vegetation or forest types may be more prevalent in areas with diverse soil compositions combined with specific slope characteristics (e.g., steep slopes supporting specific erosion-tolerant vegetation). This interaction could improve predictive performance by capturing these subtle patterns.\n\ndf["Soil_Diversity_Slope_Interaction"] = df["Soil_Diversity"] * df["Slope"]\n```end\n\n---\n\n### Dropping Columns (if necessary)\nCertain columns may offer redundant or irrelevant information. The decision to drop columns is guided by domain expertise or initial exploratory data analysis. For example:\n\n```python\n# Explanation: Hillshade indices (Hillshade_9am, Hillshade_Noon, Hillshade_3pm) often provide similar information. If feature importance evaluation indicates one is less useful, we can drop it.\ndf.drop(columns=["Hillshade_9am"], inplace=True)\n```end\n\nThese added features aim to enrich the dataset by capturing interactive effects and transformations, aligning with the domain knowledge about forest cover in relation to terrain, water drainage, human impact, and soil variability.']
