2026-05-13 01:37:26,312 - INFO - Selected best state: ("python\n# Feature 1: Elevation Relative to Vertical Distance to Hydrology\ndf['Elevation_Relative_To_Hydrology'] = df['Elevation'] - df['Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology']\n# Feature description: Calculates the elevation relative to the vertical distance from the nearest hydrological feature. This may indicate how the terrain interacts with water drainage and accumulation, which can influence forest type distribution.\n# Usefulness: Differentiates areas where elevation plays a significant role relative to nearby water features. Forest types may correlate with water availability and runoff patterns.\n\n# Feature 2: Aspect Grouped into Cardinal Directions\ndf['Aspect_Categorized'] = pd.cut(df['Aspect'], bins=[0, 90, 180, 270, 360], labels=['N', 'E', 'S', 'W'], right=False)\ndf = pd.get_dummies(df, columns=['Aspect_Categorized'], prefix='Aspect')\n# Feature description: Converts aspect (direction the slope faces) into cardinal categories (North, East, South, West) and applies one-hot encoding.\n# Usefulness: Simplifies the degrees azimuth into meaningful directional data that may correlate with microclimates or sunlight exposure, influencing vegetation growth.\n\n# Feature 3: Combined Horizontal Distance Features\ndf['Distance_To_Important_Features'] = df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology'] + df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways'] + df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points']\n# Feature description: Computes the combined horizontal distance to hydrological features, roadways, and fire points. This feature captures proximity to human activity and natural resources.\n# Usefulness: Helps understand how distance from human impacts (roadways and fire points) and natural hydrological features interact in shaping forest cover types.\nend\n\npython\n# Dropping columns with little variability\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type1', 'Soil_Type5', 'Soil_Type14', 'Soil_Type15', 'Soil_Type37'], inplace=True)\n# Explanation why the column XX is dropped: These soil type indicators have zero variance (all values are 0) across the dataset, meaning they do not provide any discriminatory information for prediction.\nend\n\npython\n# Dropping overly redundant columns\ndf.drop(columns=['Aspect'], inplace=True)\n# Explanation why the column Aspect is dropped: The raw Aspect feature has been simplified and transformed into categorized cardinal directions (one-hot encoded). Keeping the original feature would introduce redundancy and dilute the predictive power of the model.\nend\n\nBy engineering these features and dropping selected columns, we create new insights and reduce noise, further improving the downstream task of predicting forest cover type.", "### Possible Next 3 Steps for Feature Engineering\n\n1. Feature Combination: Calculate elevation difference from hydrology vertical distance\npython\n# Elevation_Hydrology_Diff\n# Feature description: Measure the absolute difference between 'Elevation' and 'Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology'. This could better capture topography interactions between elevation above water bodies or below water bodies.\n# Usefulness: This feature helps identify regions that may experience differences in terrain steepness and susceptibility to flooding or water retention, which are strongly related to forest type distributions.\ndf['Elevation_Hydrology_Diff'] = abs(df['Elevation'] - df['Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology'])\nend\n\n2. Transformation: Apply square root to 'Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways'\npython\n# Sqrt_Distance_To_Roadways\n# Feature description: Apply square root transformation to 'Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways'. This compresses high distances while preserving proximity differences for small values.\n# Usefulness: The square root transformation will emphasize areas closer to roads which can have significant human influence (e.g., urban forests, wildlife corridors). Such features may be more important in predicting forest cover near human surroundings.\ndf['Sqrt_Distance_To_Roadways'] = df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways']  0.5\nend\n\n3. Ratio: Compute ratio of horizontal distance to fire points over hydrology\npython\n# Fire_Hydrology_Ratio\n# Feature description: Calculate the ratio of 'Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points' to 'Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology'. This assesses how wildfire proximity interacts with nearby water sources, as water can act as a natural fire barrier, influencing forest compositions.\n# Usefulness: This ratio can highlight protective features (e.g., water acting as firebreaks) or vulnerabilities (e.g., dry proximity areas highly at risk for wildfires), enhancing downstream predictions.\ndf['Fire_Hydrology_Ratio'] = df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points'] / (df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology'] + 1e-5)  # Adding small constant to avoid division by zero\nend\n\n---\n\n### Dropping Unnecessary or Redundant Features\nAfter feature engineering, it is a good practice to evaluate and drop irrelevant or redundant columns. For this dataset:\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type7 is dropped\n# The column 'Soil_Type7' has a mean value of 0.0002 and a very low standard deviation (0.0127), indicating it does not exhibit significant variance across the dataset.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type7'], inplace=True)\n\n# Explanation why the column Aspect_N is dropped\n# Aspect_N contributes redundant information as it is highly binary and overlaps conceptually with soil type and elevation dynamics, which are already captured in other richer features.\ndf.drop(columns=['Aspect_N'], inplace=True)\n\n# Explanation why the column Wilderness_Area4 is dropped\n# Wilderness_Area4 has a low mean value (0.0055), with most of its values concentrated towards 0. The other wilderness area indicators seem more useful and less sparse.\ndf.drop(columns=['Wilderness_Area4'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n--- \n\n### Reasoning for Enhancements\nFeature engineering prioritizes creating meaningful abstractions that reflect critical relationships in the dataset:\n\n1. Elevation-related interactions: Features like 'Elevation_Hydrology_Diff' incorporate dynamic terrain information, providing critical insights about the interaction between elevation and water features—key elements to forest distribution.\n   \n2. Distance transformations: Applying square root or ratios refines spatial distances, enabling the model to better understand nonlinear effects that could arise from human proximity, wildfires, and hydrology.\n\n3. Feature-pruning: Columns with little variance, sparse values, or conceptual redundancy are removed. This reduces overfitting while improving model clarity by focusing on more impactful variables.\n\nThese steps align with constructing pertinent and interpretable influences critical to predicting forest cover type in geographical contexts.", "python\n# Feature name: Road_to_Fire_ratio\n# Feature description: Calculate the ratio of 'Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways' to 'Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points'.\n# Usefulness: This ratio captures the relative proximity of roadways compared to fire points. Areas closer to roadways may have been disturbed by human activity, altering forest type distribution. Furthermore, forest types may vary based on their interaction with fire risks near roadways.\ndf['Road_to_Fire_ratio'] = df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways'] / (df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points'] + 1e-6)\nend\n\npython\n# Feature name: Elevation_above_Hydrology_cubed\n# Feature description: Cube the 'Elevation_Relative_To_Hydrology' feature to amplify differences in elevation relative to nearby hydrology.\n# Usefulness: Cubing amplifies the higher values, emphasizing regions where the elevation difference with hydrology is steep. This is useful for identifying zones where water accumulation might differ drastically and could influence forest vegetation types.\ndf['Elevation_above_Hydrology_cubed'] = df['Elevation_Relative_To_Hydrology']  3\nend\n\npython\n# Feature name: Wilderness_and_Soil_interaction\n# Feature description: Create interaction terms between the wilderness areas and selected soil types (e.g., Soil_Type29 and Wilderness_Area1).\n# Usefulness: Interaction features reveal how specific soil types interact with wilderness areas, as these pairings likely capture unique terrain traits or conservation statuses that influence forest cover.\ndf['Wilderness_SOIL29_interaction'] = df['Wilderness_Area1'] * df['Soil_Type29']\ndf['Wilderness_SOIL32_interaction'] = df['Wilderness_Area3'] * df['Soil_Type32']\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Fire_Hydrology_Ratio' is dropped\n# The 'Fire_Hydrology_Ratio' feature spans a massive range with outliers skewing the distribution (Mean=8388228.1061, Std=49137594.3787). Such drastic variability prevents meaningful interactions, and it introduces noisy inputs into the model. Removing this column mitigates these challenges and streamlines preprocessing.\ndf.drop(columns=['Fire_Hydrology_Ratio'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Aspect_W' is dropped\n# The 'Aspect_W' does not provide substantial variation across the categorical values (categories = [False, True]) when compared to other directional aspects like 'Aspect_E'. Its inclusion adds redundant information that overlaps with other aspect features, and dropping it reduces feature dimensionality.\ndf.drop(columns=['Aspect_W'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Soil_Type8' is dropped\n# The proportion of instances with Soil_Type8 is exceedingly small (Mean=0.0003, Std=0.0186), indicating negligible occurrences in the dataset. Features with minimal variance rarely contribute effectively to model predictions and can increase computational complexity unnecessarily.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type8'], inplace=True)\nend"), with improvements -
